# This file tells people how to cite the pipeline. GitHub reads it and shows a
# "Cite this repository" button on the repository page.
# Format: Citation File Format 1.2.0 (https://citation-file-format.github.io/)
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this script to prepare stimuli, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "Stimulus recording pipeline: a Praat script for cutting word-list recordings into stimulus files"
version: 1.0.0
date-released: 2026-11-13
license: MIT
authors:
  - family-names: Chan
    given-names: May Pik Yu
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2365-4093"
repository-code: "https://github.com/maypychan/praat-stimulus-pipeline"
abstract: >-
  A Praat script that turns one recording of a word list into one WAV file
  per word. It segments the recording into words and pauses, checks the
  word count against a CSV of the list, labels every word from the CSV,
  and cuts each word with padding, fades and optional amplitude and
  duration normalization. It writes the labelled TextGrid and a log of
  every file it made. An optional tuning step scores segmentation settings
  against a stretch of the recording corrected by hand.
keywords:
  - Praat
  - phonetics
  - speech stimuli
  - stimulus preparation
  - word list
  - segmentation
  - TextGrid
  - psycholinguistics
  - speech perception
references:
  - type: software
    title: "Praat: doing phonetics by computer"
    authors:
      - family-names: Boersma
        given-names: Paul
      - family-names: Weenink
        given-names: David
    url: "https://www.praat.org"
